\label{sec:intro}

The asymptotic flatness of galactic rotation curves and the empirical Baryonic Tully--Fisher Relation (BTFR) \cite{McGaugh2016} have motivated extensive investigation into modified gravity, including MOND \cite{Milgrom1983} and AQUAL \cite{Bekenstein1984}. Prior work in this research program (PAPER\_01, now quarantined) proposed a single-channel arcsinh action whose Euler--Lagrange derivative yields $\operatorname{arcsinh}(x)$, not the required rational $\mu(x)$, establishing a correspondence failure.

This v1.6.0 assessment supersedes all prior working papers. It systematically delineates: what is mechanically proved $\mathbf{[P]}$, what is directly computed $\mathbf{[D]}$, what is cited from literature $\mathbf{[C]}$, and what remains open $\mathbf{[O]}$. The governing standard is the Sovereign Epistemic Covenant; the authoritative version record is \texttt{EPISTEMIC\_BOUNDARY\_v1.5.0.md}; in any conflict between this manuscript and the ledger, the ledger governs.

\label{sec:epistemic}

All claims are governed by four explicit tiers:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{[P] Proved / Machine-Verified:} Mathematical propositions mechanically checked in Lean~4 / Mathlib. Lean establishes only that statements follow from their encoded definitions; physical theory selection is not certified.
    \item \textbf{[D] Direct Empirical / Computed:} Quantities evaluated from raw, SHA-256-authenticated observational data using auditable, version-controlled scripts.
    \item \textbf{[C] Cited External Literature:} Established results from peer-reviewed sources.
    \item \textbf{[O] Open / Quarantined:} Conjectured boundary conditions, unproved physical mechanisms, or claims awaiting future derivation or data.
\end{itemize}

\noindent The complete claim-by-claim audit matrix is maintained in \texttt{CLAIM\_EVIDENCE\_LEDGER.md} and \texttt{EPISTEMIC\_BOUNDARY\_v1.5.0.md}. A human-readable referee index is in \texttt{FOR\_REFEREES.md}.

\label{sec:falsifiability}

The research program is subject to clear falsification criteria:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{CMB Power Spectrum:} If the Skordis--Z\l{}o\'snik completion with $\mathcal{J}_{\text{std}} = F_{\text{std}}(\sqrt{\mathcal{Y}})$ cannot reproduce the Planck CMB acoustic peak positions and amplitudes, the covariant embedding is falsified as a complete cosmological theory.
    \item \textbf{Structure Growth:} If the predicted matter power spectrum $P(k)$ is inconsistent with BOSS/eBOSS galaxy clustering, the model fails at cosmological scales.
    \item \textbf{Cluster Mass Budgets:} Systematic failure on galaxy cluster rotation/lensing would restrict the framework to galactic scales only.
    \item \textbf{$a_0(z)$ Redshift Test:} \textbf{Inconclusive} $\mathbf{[O]/[C]}$. The pre-registered 20-point test is withdrawn (untraceable input table), so its results are void $\mathbf{[X]}$. Real high-$z$ data (RC100, MUSE-DARK III) exclude neither constant $a_0$ nor the horizon-tied $a_0(z) = \xi c H(z)$; a calibration-controlled high-$z$ sample with a common $z=0$ estimator is what would settle it.
    \item \textbf{Lean Build Failure:} Failure of \texttt{verify\_all\_proofs.sh} on a clean clone would retract all $\mathbf{[P]}$ claims pending repair.
\end{enumerate}

\label{sec:conclusion}

This assessment establishes the following, in order of evidential weight. \textbf{Proved $\mathbf{[P]}$:} the dual-channel action $\mathcal{F}_{\text{dual}}(x)$ yields $\mu(x) = x/(1+x)$ with exact correspondence limits, and is conditionally unique under Pad\'e minimality with odds-ratio and Fisher identities --- mathematics about a branch that is now \textbf{falsified} for the solar system; the single-channel action $\mathcal{F}_{\text{single}} = x\operatorname{arcsinh}x - \sqrt{1+x^2}$ (a different function from $F_{\text{std}}$) has inverted correspondence limits; the live potential $F_{\text{std}}$ satisfies $F_{\text{std}}' = x\mu_{\text{std}}$, is strictly convex, and embeds in AeST as $\mathcal{J}_{\text{std}} = F_{\text{std}}(\sqrt{\mathcal{Y}})$; horizon-temperature matching gives $a = cH$ with the $2\pi$ cancelling; and pure RAQUAL and the disformal completion are excluded. \textbf{Derived or computed $\mathbf{[D]}$:} the AeST completion has $c_T = c$ structurally and no superluminal mode on Minkowski space at the published parameter points; the SPARC extraction under $\mu_{\text{std}}$ gives $a_0 = 1.161\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ (95\% interval $[0.972, 1.295]\times10^{-10}$, 175 galaxies); and the Cassini external-field quadrupole excludes bare $\mu_{\text{std}}$ at $\approx 4.6\sigma$.

Three earlier results of this programme are withdrawn or narrowed, and we state them plainly. The solar-system safety claim ($Q_2 \approx 4.9\times10^{-29}\,\mathrm{s^{-2}}$, a factor $\sim70$ below the Cassini bound) and the ``sharpest prediction'' of a residual $Q_2 \sim 10^{-29}\,\mathrm{s^{-2}}$ rested on $\mu(x) = x/(1+x)$ and on a Vainshtein screening that the $c_T = c$-safe sector cannot supply; both are retracted $\mathbf{[X]}$ (\S\ref{sec:solar}). The linear screening of structure growth to $0.23\%$ was computed with $\mathcal{F}_{\text{dual}}$'s ratio $\mathcal{F}''/\mathcal{F}'$ and has not been recomputed for $\mu_{\text{std}}$; it is conditional. The $a_0$ value $1.116\times10^{-10}$ and its $0.46\sigma$ agreement with the horizon anchor were extracted under $\mu_{\text{dual}}$ on a superseded 171-galaxy sample and are replaced by the value above; inverted through the declared $a_0 = cH_0/2\pi$, it corresponds to $H_0 \approx 71$--$75\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}\,Mpc^{-1}}$, a consistency check that depends on that declared normalisation.

The open problems are of three kinds. \emph{Structural:} which AeST branch (held or dragged) a given system occupies, a covariant realization of the screening that the Cassini quadrupole requires, and the origin of the $1/(2\pi)$ normalisation. \emph{Computational:} non-linear structure formation, the $\mu_{\text{std}}$ screening ratio on FLRW backgrounds, and a Boltzmann calculation that does not rely on unpublished AeST initial conditions. \emph{Observational:} a calibration-controlled high-$z$ kinematic sample --- the pre-registered $a_0(z)$ test is withdrawn (untraceable input table), and the real RC100 and MUSE-DARK III data exclude neither a constant nor a horizon-tied $a_0$ --- and next-generation ranging. The Cassini quadrupole is the sharpest test the theory currently faces, and bare $\mu_{\text{std}}$ does not pass it.

